      "group": "massing-and-roof",
      "binding": "open",
      "status": "drafted",
      "confidence": "medium",
      "judgment": true,
      "note": "Deliberately left open, not specified: three of the four members (french-baroque, french-neoclassical, beaux-arts-french) state 'strict-bilateral' in their own proportional_system.symmetry, but the fourth and earliest, french-renaissance-chateau, states 'near-symmetrical' — its own paradigm exemplar, Chambord, is topped by 'an asymmetrical roofscape of eleven tower types and three chimney types arranged with no symmetry whatsoever.' Forcing strict-bilateral here would silently overwrite a real, sourced difference in the earliest member.",
      "rule": "Symmetry class differs by period within the family and is resolved at the style level, not here.",
      "variants": [
        {
          "id": "strict-bilateral-symmetry",
          "status": "permitted",
          "note": "french-baroque, french-neoclassical and beaux-arts-french all state this identically in their own proportional_system.symmetry."
        },
        {
          "id": "near-symmetrical-with-heraldic-irregularity",
          "status": "permitted",
          "note": "french-renaissance-chateau's own proportional_system.symmetry is literally 'near-symmetrical'; its roofscape is explicitly not symmetrized even where the wall below is."
        }
      ]
    },
